      // [src_riemann_mactutor, src_riemann_dsb, src_riemann_zeta1859 | 1859 | university biography + reference work + own paper] MacTutor (quoting Klein, who heard it from Weierstrass) says Riemann fully recognised the justice of Weierstrass's critique of the Dirichlet principle but maintained that his existence theorems remained correct; Ullrich's postscript reports his 1859 remark that he had used the principle only as a convenient tool, and the zeta paper openly says a stricter proof is wanted. He accepted the criticism of the method without abandoning the claims, a mixed signal. A single episode relayed second-hand, so inference.
      belief_updating: [58, 0.48, "i", "N"],
      // [src_riemann_mactutor, src_riemann_dedekind | 1851-1857 | university biography + friend's biography] MacTutor says he spent thirty months on the habilitation dissertation, and Dedekind, who attended the 1855-56 lectures, describes how Riemann worked on the abelian functions investigations begun in 1851-52 and, once Borchardt asked for them, devoted all his powers to writing them up in 1857. Sustained multi-year concentration on single problems is documented, but by one friend and one secondary biography; nothing from his private or health life is used.
      deep_focus: [70, 0.55, "s", "A"],
      // [src_riemann_mactutor, src_riemann_dsb, src_riemann_dedekind | 1849-1860 | university biography + reference work + friend's biography] Riemann was Wilhelm Weber's assistant in physics, drew topology ideas from Listing, prepared two of his three habilitation lectures on electricity, reported on static electricity to a Göttingen society, and published on the propagation of finite-amplitude sound waves (Freudenthal), alongside complex analysis, number theory, Fourier series and geometry. His work spans pure analysis and mathematical physics, though all within the mathematical sciences, hence a moderate score.
      cross_domain_range: [66, 0.55, "d", "A"],
      // [src_riemann_dedekind, src_riemann_mactutor | 1840s-1850s | friend's biography + university biography] Dedekind writes that his shyness never wholly left him and often led him to withdraw into solitude and thought; MacTutor says he gradually overcame a natural shyness in lecturing to a small class of eight students. Two sources agree on a reserved manner, but both rely on the same family letters in Dedekind's account, so they are not fully independent and the rating is a moderate lean.
      social_assertiveness: [36, 0.5, "s", "N"],
