      // [src_le_ciocloud, src_le_dmaverick, src_le_commoncog | 1983-2011 | news items + business-history essay] In September 2008 he called cloud computing 'complete gibberish' and said Oracle would change little beyond some advertising wording; in October 2011 he announced Oracle's own public cloud service, which the Daily Maverick writer notes came not three years later; the essay records an earlier shift when the database was rewritten for portability and when the company moved from consulting to product. A real course change with a late start, shown by two news items and a critical commentary, hence a mid score with dual-edged impact and low confidence.
      adaptability: [50, 0.42, "d", "D"],
      // [src_le_commoncog, src_le_philstar | 1977-1980 | business-history essay + keynote report] The essay says SDL won a subcontract with a bid a third of the next lowest, funded itself without venture equity, and stopped paying some salaries, starting with Ellison's, when government payment lagged six to nine months; Ellison's keynote repeats the first CIA sale and the nine-month wait. Both sources trace to Ellison's own account (the essay draws on his authorised biography), so one perspective; confidence capped accordingly.
