      // [src_rf_rgs, src_rf_spri, src_rf_bristol, src_rf_pw, src_rf_lrb | 1977-2009 | archive record + polar-institute biography + university citation + book review + magazine review] The RGS record lists a run of long expeditions from 1977 to 1993, the Transglobe Expedition taking seven years of preparation and three of travel (London Review of Books); Publishers Weekly says that in 1992-93 each man hauled a sleigh of nearly 500 pounds and kept going until day 95, when they were too weak to continue; the polar-research biography records unsupported North Pole attempts in 1986, 1990 and 2000 that failed, and Bristol records Everest attempts in 2005 and 2008 before the 2009 summit. Repeated return after failed or curtailed attempts is documented by an archive, two institutions and two press pieces, including a sceptical one, so documented at a fairly high confidence. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): reviewed as the highest-scoring row; kept because five independent sources agree on the pattern of repeated attempts and none disputes it.
