      risk_tolerance: [70, 0.58, "d", "D"],
      // [src_rf_rgs, src_rf_lrb | 1977-1982 | archive record + magazine review] The RGS record lists a 1977 preliminary approach toward the North Pole before the Transglobe Expedition, and the papers he donated include a stores book of planned sledge loads and hut plans, a sledge load plan, a navigation notebook and a planning map for the North West Passage; the London Review of Books review says preparation took seven years, that he picked people for good nature, patience and fitness and had the Territorial Army train them in the needed skills, and that he secured Ministry of Defence support. Two independent sources show long, staged preparation, but the archive entry is a catalogue summary and the review is secondhand on the planning detail, so documented at a moderate confidence.
      planning_orientation: [68, 0.58, "d", "A"],
      // [src_rf_lrb, src_rf_rgs, src_rf_csm | 1969-2013 | magazine review + archive record + wire report] The London Review of Books review describes expeditions financed through sponsors and media rights, a televised parachute drop for a Norwegian glacier survey, free training from the Territorial Army and military help including an RAF Hercules flight and an army Land Rover, and calls his talent for getting others to fund his ventures 'singular'; the RGS record lists the Transglobe papers and newsletters; the Associated Press report shows the 2013 expedition raising $10 million for a charity. Documented across decades by a sceptical reviewer and a wire report, dual-edged because the same reviewer says the BBC films left viewers thinking him a publicity-seeker and the dependence on sponsors drew criticism; moderate confidence because the fullest detail is from a single 1988 review.
